1
00:00:01,919 --> 00:00:03,807
Hey there, and welcome!

2
00:00:03,978 --> 00:00:06,878
I'm Ava, and in this guide I'll show you,

3
00:00:06,919 --> 00:00:10,821
step by step, how to install and run Jasmin Web Panel,

4
00:00:10,821 --> 00:00:14,331
the free and open source control center for Jasmin SMS

5
00:00:14,331 --> 00:00:15,261
Gateway.

6
00:00:15,481 --> 00:00:18,100
First, we'll see how it all works.

7
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:20,539
Then we'll install it five different ways:

8
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:23,481
with one single command, with Docker Compose,

9
00:00:23,481 --> 00:00:25,994
as native systemd services behind Nginx,

10
00:00:25,994 --> 00:00:30,223
on Kubernetes, and from Debian and RPM packages.

11
00:00:30,231 --> 00:00:33,271
After that, we'll create a first customer,

12
00:00:33,271 --> 00:00:36,336
connect a carrier, send a real test message,

13
00:00:36,336 --> 00:00:38,842
and lock everything down for production.

14
00:00:38,983 --> 00:00:41,478
So grab a coffee, and let's dive in!

15
00:00:43,072 --> 00:00:44,246
Chapter one!

16
00:00:44,246 --> 00:00:45,509
Let's see how it all works,

17
00:00:45,509 --> 00:00:47,436
and what you need before we start.

18
00:00:48,872 --> 00:00:51,917
So, what exactly is Jasmin Web Panel?

19
00:00:51,938 --> 00:00:56,122
It's a Django application that manages Jasmin SMS Gateway for

20
00:00:56,122 --> 00:00:56,838
you.

21
00:00:56,859 --> 00:01:00,043
Every action you take, in the browser or through the REST

22
00:01:00,043 --> 00:01:02,627
API, becomes a jcli command,

23
00:01:02,701 --> 00:01:06,753
sent to Jasmin's management console on port 8990,

24
00:01:06,753 --> 00:01:10,864
and followed by persist, so your changes survive restarts.

25
00:01:11,013 --> 00:01:13,067
And here's the part I love:

26
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:16,328
your SMS traffic never flows through the panel.

27
00:01:16,328 --> 00:01:20,331
It stays inside Jasmin, so the panel adds zero latency to

28
00:01:20,331 --> 00:01:21,779
message delivery.

29
00:01:24,325 --> 00:01:27,410
Before installing, let's check the environment.

30
00:01:27,431 --> 00:01:28,681
You'll need a Linux server,

31
00:01:28,681 --> 00:01:32,089
like Ubuntu 22.04 or newer,

32
00:01:32,089 --> 00:01:34,686
Debian 12, or Rocky Linux 9,

33
00:01:34,694 --> 00:01:36,883
with at least two gigabytes of memory.

34
00:01:36,891 --> 00:01:39,857
The panel uses PostgreSQL for its data,

35
00:01:39,996 --> 00:01:42,483
Redis for caching and background jobs,

36
00:01:42,483 --> 00:01:45,383
and of course, a running Jasmin gateway.

37
00:01:45,555 --> 00:01:48,391
Good news: if you choose Docker Compose,

38
00:01:48,509 --> 00:01:50,904
all of these are started for you automatically.

39
00:01:53,446 --> 00:01:55,582
There are five ways to install.

40
00:01:55,603 --> 00:01:57,972
The one-line installer is the fastest,

41
00:01:57,972 --> 00:02:00,306
and works on most distributions.

42
00:02:00,327 --> 00:02:03,898
Docker Compose gives you the whole stack in containers.

43
00:02:03,919 --> 00:02:07,701
Native systemd services suit classic servers.

44
00:02:07,735 --> 00:02:10,525
Kubernetes manifests are ready for your clusters,

45
00:02:10,525 --> 00:02:15,464
and Debian or RPM packages fit configuration managed fleets.

46
00:02:15,617 --> 00:02:18,648
Just pick the chapter that matches your infrastructure.

47
00:02:18,648 --> 00:02:20,727
Each one stands on its own.

48
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:25,431
Chapter two.

49
00:02:25,431 --> 00:02:27,098
The one-line installer.

50
00:02:27,098 --> 00:02:29,712
Honestly, this one is my favorite!

51
00:02:31,142 --> 00:02:31,988
Okay!

52
00:02:31,988 --> 00:02:34,762
The universal installer is a single script.

53
00:02:34,899 --> 00:02:36,694
It detects your distribution,

54
00:02:36,702 --> 00:02:38,623
installs the base packages,

55
00:02:38,623 --> 00:02:41,439
finds the latest release, and then asks you just two

56
00:02:41,439 --> 00:02:44,347
questions: Docker or systemd,

57
00:02:44,347 --> 00:02:46,062
and your domain name.

58
00:02:46,163 --> 00:02:48,024
One important habit, though.

59
00:02:48,062 --> 00:02:51,538
Always review a script before piping it to bash.

60
00:02:51,573 --> 00:02:53,891
Download it first, and read it,

61
00:02:53,891 --> 00:02:55,260
just like this.

62
00:02:57,818 --> 00:03:01,700
For automation, like cloud init or CI pipelines,

63
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:05,503
pass the options as flags, and add non interactive.

64
00:03:05,503 --> 00:03:08,753
This example installs the full Docker stack for the domain

65
00:03:08,753 --> 00:03:11,337
sms dot example dot com.

66
00:03:11,464 --> 00:03:13,424
And look at that, it's healthy already!

67
00:03:13,584 --> 00:03:16,939
The installer generated a fifty character secret key and a

68
00:03:16,939 --> 00:03:18,878
separate field encryption key,

69
00:03:18,912 --> 00:03:21,681
saved them to a protected environment file,

70
00:03:21,689 --> 00:03:24,826
and bound the jcli port to localhost only.

71
00:03:24,969 --> 00:03:28,557
Just remember to change that default login right away.

72
00:03:31,125 --> 00:03:34,973
Prefer a native install with automatic HTTPS?

73
00:03:34,981 --> 00:03:39,881
Choose systemd mode, and add with SSL and your email address.

74
00:03:39,902 --> 00:03:42,446
The script installs PostgreSQL,

75
00:03:42,573 --> 00:03:46,191
Redis and Nginx, creates a dedicated system user,

76
00:03:46,191 --> 00:03:49,723
provisions Python 3.12 with uv,

77
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:53,310
runs the migrations, installs hardened systemd units,

78
00:03:53,310 --> 00:03:56,749
and requests a free Let's Encrypt certificate.

79
00:03:56,825 --> 00:03:59,156
All of that, in one command!

80
00:03:59,305 --> 00:04:02,534
Just make sure your domain already points to this server.

81
00:04:05,018 --> 00:04:08,234
Run the script with help to see every option.

82
00:04:08,243 --> 00:04:10,401
You can pin a release with the version flag,

83
00:04:10,401 --> 00:04:14,256
change the web port, or point systemd mode at an existing

84
00:04:14,256 --> 00:04:15,656
Jasmin host.

85
00:04:15,797 --> 00:04:18,903
Running the installer again simply upgrades in place,

86
00:04:18,903 --> 00:04:20,856
and keeps your configuration.

87
00:04:20,997 --> 00:04:24,003
And the uninstall flag removes the application,

88
00:04:24,010 --> 00:04:26,853
but carefully preserves your database and your environment

89
00:04:26,853 --> 00:04:27,713
file.

90
00:04:31,318 --> 00:04:32,665
Chapter three.

91
00:04:32,669 --> 00:04:36,516
Docker Compose, the complete stack in about two minutes.

92
00:04:37,912 --> 00:04:42,018
Docker Compose is the quickest way to run everything on one

93
00:04:42,018 --> 00:04:43,004
host.

94
00:04:43,004 --> 00:04:47,010
Clone the repository, and copy the sample environment file.

95
00:04:47,018 --> 00:04:49,346
Then generate a strong secret key,

96
00:04:49,346 --> 00:04:51,268
and a field encryption key,

97
00:04:51,268 --> 00:04:53,589
with Python's secrets module.

98
00:04:53,707 --> 00:04:57,408
Please, never keep the sample values in production.

99
00:04:57,443 --> 00:05:00,498
The panel will actually warn you if it detects them.

100
00:05:03,059 --> 00:05:05,782
Now, start the stack with docker compose up,

101
00:05:05,782 --> 00:05:07,288
in detached mode.

102
00:05:07,414 --> 00:05:08,474
And boom!

103
00:05:08,474 --> 00:05:11,671
Seven services, all up and running!

104
00:05:11,825 --> 00:05:14,956
The web panel, a Celery worker for exports,

105
00:05:14,956 --> 00:05:18,462
PostgreSQL, Redis, RabbitMQ,

106
00:05:18,469 --> 00:05:22,383
the Jasmin gateway itself, and the submit log collector.

107
00:05:22,538 --> 00:05:25,969
The panel runs its database migrations on every start,

108
00:05:26,023 --> 00:05:28,974
so upgrades need no manual steps at all.

109
00:05:31,492 --> 00:05:33,458
Let's check the health endpoint.

110
00:05:33,479 --> 00:05:37,129
It needs no login, and returns the running version.

111
00:05:37,221 --> 00:05:38,560
Status, okay.

112
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:39,490
Perfect.

113
00:05:39,732 --> 00:05:43,798
Follow the web logs while you open the panel on port 8999 in

114
00:05:43,798 --> 00:05:44,856
your browser.

115
00:05:44,877 --> 00:05:47,659
And if Jasmin already runs somewhere else,

116
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:49,803
you can start just the panel container,

117
00:05:49,825 --> 00:05:52,974
and point the telnet host variable at your gateway.

118
00:05:55,546 --> 00:06:00,124
Before any upgrade, always take a database backup with pg

119
00:06:00,124 --> 00:06:00,960
dump.

120
00:06:01,083 --> 00:06:04,049
Then, upgrading takes just two commands:

121
00:06:04,070 --> 00:06:07,273
pull the new images, and recreate the containers.

122
00:06:07,333 --> 00:06:09,215
Jasmin's own configuration,

123
00:06:09,215 --> 00:06:11,917
including users, connectors and routes,

124
00:06:11,952 --> 00:06:15,075
lives in the jasmin config named volume.

125
00:06:15,185 --> 00:06:18,666
So it safely survives container recreation too.

126
00:06:22,289 --> 00:06:23,618
Chapter four.

127
00:06:23,618 --> 00:06:26,662
A classic native install, with systemd,

128
00:06:26,683 --> 00:06:29,491
Nginx, and free HTTPS.

129
00:06:30,939 --> 00:06:33,031
For a manual native installation,

130
00:06:33,031 --> 00:06:34,970
start with the system packages:

131
00:06:35,044 --> 00:06:39,294
PostgreSQL, Redis, Nginx, and the telnet client,

132
00:06:39,294 --> 00:06:41,957
which the panel uses to talk to jcli.

133
00:06:41,965 --> 00:06:46,010
Then create a database, and a dedicated database user.

134
00:06:46,124 --> 00:06:49,235
On PostgreSQL 15 and newer,

135
00:06:49,340 --> 00:06:53,462
don't forget to grant usage and create on the public schema.

136
00:06:53,498 --> 00:06:56,482
Otherwise, the migrations will fail.

137
00:06:58,959 --> 00:07:02,485
Next, create a system user without a login shell,

138
00:07:02,485 --> 00:07:05,261
clone the code into opt jasmin web panel,

139
00:07:05,261 --> 00:07:10,464
and build an isolated Python 3.12 environment with uv.

140
00:07:10,588 --> 00:07:14,447
The nice thing is, uv downloads a managed Python for you.

141
00:07:14,464 --> 00:07:18,387
No extra repositories, and no compiling from source,

142
00:07:18,504 --> 00:07:20,546
even on older distributions.

143
00:07:23,072 --> 00:07:25,538
Keep the configuration outside the code,

144
00:07:25,598 --> 00:07:28,472
in etc jasmin web panel env,

145
00:07:28,717 --> 00:07:31,085
readable only by the service user.

146
00:07:31,085 --> 00:07:34,348
Set the secret keys, the database URL,

147
00:07:34,348 --> 00:07:38,130
the allowed hosts, and the jcli credentials of your gateway.

148
00:07:38,138 --> 00:07:41,177
Then run the migrations, collect static files,

149
00:07:41,177 --> 00:07:43,696
and create your administrator account.

150
00:07:43,818 --> 00:07:45,409
Superuser created.

151
00:07:45,409 --> 00:07:46,857
We're getting close!

152
00:07:49,392 --> 00:07:53,195
The repository ships hardened unit files for Gunicorn and for

153
00:07:53,195 --> 00:07:54,608
the Celery worker.

154
00:07:54,616 --> 00:07:56,713
They run as the dedicated user,

155
00:07:56,761 --> 00:08:00,397
load the environment file, and use systemd sandboxing,

156
00:08:00,405 --> 00:08:03,134
like protect system and private temp.

157
00:08:03,155 --> 00:08:06,839
Copy them, reload systemd, and enable both services,

158
00:08:06,839 --> 00:08:08,358
so they start at boot.

159
00:08:08,473 --> 00:08:09,963
Active, and running.

160
00:08:09,963 --> 00:08:10,969
Lovely.

161
00:08:13,591 --> 00:08:16,018
Finally, let's put Nginx in front.

162
00:08:16,052 --> 00:08:18,636
Use the template in the packaging folder,

163
00:08:18,670 --> 00:08:21,315
fill in the server name and the install directory,

164
00:08:21,315 --> 00:08:24,452
enable the site, and test the configuration.

165
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:26,578
Certbot then grabs a certificate,

166
00:08:26,578 --> 00:08:30,070
and redirects HTTP to HTTPS.

167
00:08:30,217 --> 00:08:33,450
And just like that, you have HTTPS!

168
00:08:33,602 --> 00:08:37,668
Last step: set HTTPS only to true,

169
00:08:37,703 --> 00:08:41,516
and add your domain to the CSRF trusted origins.

170
00:08:45,067 --> 00:08:46,305
Chapter five.

171
00:08:46,305 --> 00:08:47,519
Kubernetes!

172
00:08:47,519 --> 00:08:49,276
Let's go cloud native.

173
00:08:50,725 --> 00:08:54,441
The repository includes a ready-made Kustomize bundle,

174
00:08:54,462 --> 00:08:56,625
in the deploy kubernetes folder.

175
00:08:56,633 --> 00:08:58,633
Start by creating the namespace,

176
00:08:58,633 --> 00:09:01,218
and a secret with your database URL,

177
00:09:01,252 --> 00:09:04,639
your jcli credentials, and two random keys.

178
00:09:04,764 --> 00:09:07,116
Keep your secrets out of Git.

179
00:09:07,333 --> 00:09:10,518
For GitOps, use a tool like Sealed Secrets,

180
00:09:10,518 --> 00:09:12,470
or External Secrets.

181
00:09:14,992 --> 00:09:17,308
Edit the config map with your domain,

182
00:09:17,308 --> 00:09:20,958
and the service names of Jasmin and Redis in your cluster.

183
00:09:20,966 --> 00:09:24,216
Then apply the whole bundle with kubectl apply,

184
00:09:24,216 --> 00:09:25,326
dash k.

185
00:09:25,490 --> 00:09:28,811
A smart detail: an init container runs the database

186
00:09:28,811 --> 00:09:31,825
migrations once, before the web pods start,

187
00:09:31,879 --> 00:09:35,259
so several replicas never race each other at boot.

188
00:09:35,363 --> 00:09:37,049
Successfully rolled out!

189
00:09:39,559 --> 00:09:43,051
The ingress uses ingress nginx with cert manager,

190
00:09:43,072 --> 00:09:46,248
to issue a TLS certificate automatically.

191
00:09:46,256 --> 00:09:49,801
Liveness and readiness probes call the health check endpoint,

192
00:09:49,888 --> 00:09:52,888
and the pods run without privilege escalation,

193
00:09:52,888 --> 00:09:54,564
with resource limits.

194
00:09:54,697 --> 00:09:56,161
Need more capacity?

195
00:09:56,161 --> 00:09:58,447
Scale the web tier horizontally,

196
00:09:58,447 --> 00:09:59,859
with one command.

197
00:10:02,372 --> 00:10:04,767
To upgrade, change the image tag,

198
00:10:04,767 --> 00:10:07,719
for example with kubectl set image,

199
00:10:07,727 --> 00:10:10,575
or with a Kustomize image override.

200
00:10:10,609 --> 00:10:13,575
Kubernetes then performs a rolling update,

201
00:10:13,635 --> 00:10:15,430
with zero downtime.

202
00:10:15,578 --> 00:10:17,547
And if anything goes wrong,

203
00:10:17,547 --> 00:10:18,608
relax.

204
00:10:18,608 --> 00:10:21,584
You can roll back to the previous revision instantly.

205
00:10:25,218 --> 00:10:26,581
Chapter six.

206
00:10:26,581 --> 00:10:29,283
Native Debian and RPM packages,

207
00:10:29,283 --> 00:10:31,123
perfect for server fleets.

208
00:10:32,572 --> 00:10:36,230
Every tagged release also publishes native Debian and RPM

209
00:10:36,230 --> 00:10:40,388
packages, for amd64 and arm64,

210
00:10:40,388 --> 00:10:42,361
together with a checksum file.

211
00:10:42,361 --> 00:10:45,756
On Debian or Ubuntu, download the package,

212
00:10:45,756 --> 00:10:47,669
and verify its checksum.

213
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:49,589
It says OK, great!

214
00:10:49,736 --> 00:10:53,105
Now install it with apt, which resolves dependencies like

215
00:10:53,105 --> 00:10:57,044
Python, lib pq and telnet automatically.

216
00:10:59,559 --> 00:11:03,288
On Rocky Linux, AlmaLinux, RHEL or Fedora,

217
00:11:03,348 --> 00:11:06,196
the flow is the same, with dnf.

218
00:11:06,363 --> 00:11:08,947
And look at everything the package set up for you:

219
00:11:09,103 --> 00:11:11,098
the jasmin panel system user,

220
00:11:11,098 --> 00:11:13,610
the systemd units, the Python environment,

221
00:11:13,610 --> 00:11:15,967
and even a unique secret key,

222
00:11:16,047 --> 00:11:18,101
generated on first install.

223
00:11:18,223 --> 00:11:21,689
Upgrades never overwrite your existing configuration.

224
00:11:24,239 --> 00:11:26,086
After installing a package,

225
00:11:26,252 --> 00:11:29,323
edit the environment file to set your database URL,

226
00:11:29,475 --> 00:11:32,389
allowed hosts, and jcli credentials.

227
00:11:32,410 --> 00:11:35,625
Run the migrations, create an administrator,

228
00:11:35,673 --> 00:11:37,665
and enable the services.

229
00:11:37,673 --> 00:11:40,146
An Nginx template is installed right next to the

230
00:11:40,146 --> 00:11:43,778
configuration, so you can publish the panel exactly like in

231
00:11:43,778 --> 00:11:45,375
the systemd chapter.

232
00:11:48,933 --> 00:11:50,171
Chapter seven.

233
00:11:50,171 --> 00:11:52,338
This is where the magic happens!

234
00:11:52,338 --> 00:11:53,862
Let's configure the gateway,

235
00:11:53,862 --> 00:11:56,274
and send our very first message.

236
00:11:57,725 --> 00:12:01,139
Open the panel, and sign in with the bootstrap account:

237
00:12:01,357 --> 00:12:04,310
admin, with the password, secret.

238
00:12:04,419 --> 00:12:06,729
Now, change it immediately,

239
00:12:06,877 --> 00:12:08,906
either from your profile page,

240
00:12:09,040 --> 00:12:10,929
or from the command line.

241
00:12:11,080 --> 00:12:14,283
New passwords must pass Django's validators,

242
00:12:14,291 --> 00:12:17,296
so short or common passwords are rejected.

243
00:12:17,304 --> 00:12:21,001
And the default admin is only ever created on an empty

244
00:12:21,001 --> 00:12:22,112
database.

245
00:12:24,655 --> 00:12:27,559
Everything in the user interface is also available through

246
00:12:27,559 --> 00:12:31,040
the REST API, which is fantastic for automation.

247
00:12:31,164 --> 00:12:33,314
Only staff accounts can call it.

248
00:12:33,401 --> 00:12:35,941
Here, we create a customers group,

249
00:12:35,941 --> 00:12:38,722
and a Jasmin user named acme.

250
00:12:38,756 --> 00:12:41,446
These are gateway users who send SMS,

251
00:12:41,520 --> 00:12:44,406
separate from the panel's own login accounts.

252
00:12:46,939 --> 00:12:50,154
Next, let's connect to your SMS provider.

253
00:12:50,175 --> 00:12:54,018
Create an SMPP client connector with the carrier's host,

254
00:12:54,018 --> 00:12:57,023
port and credentials, and then start it.

255
00:12:57,126 --> 00:13:00,019
Look, the session shows bound transceiver.

256
00:13:00,019 --> 00:13:01,145
We're connected!

257
00:13:01,294 --> 00:13:03,883
And notice that the carrier password is masked,

258
00:13:03,883 --> 00:13:06,278
in every API response.

259
00:13:08,839 --> 00:13:12,686
Now, let's tell Jasmin where to send messages.

260
00:13:12,707 --> 00:13:16,778
A default MT route sends all traffic through carrier one,

261
00:13:16,839 --> 00:13:18,739
at a rate of one cent.

262
00:13:18,760 --> 00:13:21,668
Then we add a destination filter for numbers starting with

263
00:13:21,668 --> 00:13:25,799
971, and a static route with a higher order,

264
00:13:25,799 --> 00:13:28,423
so those messages use a cheaper rate.

265
00:13:28,502 --> 00:13:33,256
Remember, routes with a higher order are evaluated first.

266
00:13:35,738 --> 00:13:38,198
Okay, the moment of truth!

267
00:13:38,329 --> 00:13:42,987
Let's send a real test SMS through Jasmin's HTTP API,

268
00:13:42,987 --> 00:13:46,782
on port 1401, using the acme credentials.

269
00:13:46,881 --> 00:13:49,155
Success, with a message ID!

270
00:13:49,155 --> 00:13:51,996
Our very first message is on its way!

271
00:13:52,165 --> 00:13:54,867
You can also use the Send SMS page in the panel,

272
00:13:54,867 --> 00:13:58,398
which supports both HTTP and SMPP,

273
00:13:58,415 --> 00:14:00,936
including long multipart messages.

274
00:14:04,496 --> 00:14:05,882
Chapter eight.

275
00:14:05,917 --> 00:14:07,903
The Submit Log, monitoring,

276
00:14:07,903 --> 00:14:09,290
and troubleshooting.

277
00:14:10,725 --> 00:14:13,318
The submit log records every message,

278
00:14:13,318 --> 00:14:16,573
with its status, cost, and delivery report.

279
00:14:16,581 --> 00:14:21,678
In Jasmin's configuration, enable publish submit sm resp,

280
00:14:21,739 --> 00:14:23,757
in the sm listener section,

281
00:14:23,765 --> 00:14:25,791
and restart Jasmin.

282
00:14:25,791 --> 00:14:29,007
Then, in the panel, set submit log to true.

283
00:14:29,155 --> 00:14:32,526
With Docker Compose, the sms logger service is already

284
00:14:32,526 --> 00:14:35,401
running, and ready to receive messages.

285
00:14:37,922 --> 00:14:40,392
Now the fun part, the data!

286
00:14:40,530 --> 00:14:42,978
On the dashboard, you see total,

287
00:14:42,978 --> 00:14:45,465
successful and failed messages,

288
00:14:45,465 --> 00:14:48,088
with a timeline by day, week,

289
00:14:48,162 --> 00:14:49,838
month, or year.

290
00:14:49,859 --> 00:14:53,128
The submit log page lets you search by message ID,

291
00:14:53,175 --> 00:14:57,912
address, user, or content, filter by status and date,

292
00:14:57,912 --> 00:15:02,101
and export the results to CSV or Excel in the background.

293
00:15:02,234 --> 00:15:06,329
And exports stay private, to the user who requested them.

294
00:15:08,863 --> 00:15:11,419
If something goes wrong, don't panic.

295
00:15:11,562 --> 00:15:14,054
If a page says it cannot connect to jcli,

296
00:15:14,128 --> 00:15:15,949
check that Jasmin is running,

297
00:15:15,957 --> 00:15:19,167
and that port 8990 is reachable from the panel,

298
00:15:19,167 --> 00:15:20,265
with telnet.

299
00:15:20,286 --> 00:15:24,391
Login failures mean the telnet username or password is wrong.

300
00:15:24,391 --> 00:15:28,062
Permission denied for schema public means the PostgreSQL

301
00:15:28,062 --> 00:15:29,660
grant is missing.

302
00:15:29,799 --> 00:15:33,179
And the service logs always include the exact reason.

303
00:15:36,796 --> 00:15:38,151
Chapter nine.

304
00:15:38,200 --> 00:15:39,934
Production security.

305
00:15:39,970 --> 00:15:41,957
Please, don't skip this one.

306
00:15:43,432 --> 00:15:46,802
Before going live, review your application settings

307
00:15:46,802 --> 00:15:47,820
carefully.

308
00:15:47,870 --> 00:15:49,829
Debug must be false.

309
00:15:49,953 --> 00:15:53,124
Use long random secret and encryption keys,

310
00:15:53,124 --> 00:15:56,269
and set the field encryption key before you ever rotate the

311
00:15:56,269 --> 00:15:57,399
secret key.

312
00:15:57,399 --> 00:15:59,794
Restrict the allowed hosts to your domain,

313
00:15:59,794 --> 00:16:03,057
enable HTTPS only behind TLS,

314
00:16:03,057 --> 00:16:06,439
and keep the risky EvalPy filter disabled,

315
00:16:06,439 --> 00:16:08,155
unless you truly need it.

316
00:16:10,555 --> 00:16:12,457
Next, the network.

317
00:16:12,508 --> 00:16:15,071
jcli is a plain text protocol,

318
00:16:15,121 --> 00:16:17,431
with full control of your gateway.

319
00:16:17,494 --> 00:16:22,599
So never, ever expose port 8990 to the internet.

320
00:16:22,717 --> 00:16:25,269
Keep it on localhost, a private network,

321
00:16:25,269 --> 00:16:26,498
or a VPN.

322
00:16:26,519 --> 00:16:30,169
Change the default RabbitMQ and database passwords,

323
00:16:30,243 --> 00:16:32,783
give panel access only to staff you trust,

324
00:16:32,783 --> 00:16:35,801
and keep the panel and its images up to date.

325
00:16:35,907 --> 00:16:38,605
Do this, and you can sleep well at night.

326
00:16:42,329 --> 00:16:43,800
And that's a wrap!

327
00:16:43,950 --> 00:16:46,462
You now have Jasmin Web Panel installed,

328
00:16:46,462 --> 00:16:48,933
secured, and sending messages.

329
00:16:48,937 --> 00:16:50,499
Honestly, great job.

330
00:16:50,697 --> 00:16:54,395
The full documentation, with every configuration option and

331
00:16:54,395 --> 00:16:58,242
the REST API reference, is linked in the description.

332
00:16:58,290 --> 00:17:01,540
Jasmin Web Panel is free and open source,

333
00:17:01,540 --> 00:17:03,413
under the MIT license.

334
00:17:03,524 --> 00:17:06,655
If it helps you, please star the repository on GitHub,

335
00:17:06,655 --> 00:17:08,133
it really means a lot.

336
00:17:08,133 --> 00:17:11,373
And come say hi in our Telegram community.

337
00:17:11,524 --> 00:17:14,787
And if your business needs an enterprise grade platform with

338
00:17:14,787 --> 00:17:18,129
professional support, take a look at SMPP Forge,

339
00:17:18,129 --> 00:17:20,055
at smppforge dot com.

340
00:17:20,212 --> 00:17:21,820
Thanks so much for watching,

341
00:17:21,820 --> 00:17:23,607
and happy messaging!
